@coderabbitai Valid. Fixed in 6a6245235f: when `next_limit` is already 1, the pre-sizing marker now records the head candidate (`SELECT id ... ORDER BY time_updated, time_created, id LIMIT 1` over the same `candidate_filter` the sizing query uses). Recovery then sees a single ID at the floor and quarantines it via the existing `_archive_oversized_record` path, exactly as for a killed write.

The ID is recorded only at `next_limit == 1`, matching the write marker (which records `next_limit`), so a session is quarantined only after a kill at the persisted 1-row floor, not on the first kill under load.

Runtime evidence (isolated fixture, persisted limits `1`, sqlite3 interposer that hangs on the `WITH cand AS` sizing query, 5s budget): killed pass rc=2 with marker `1 67108864 <pid> ses-1183`; next run warns "Session ses-1183 still interrupted a 1-session batch; deferring it as oversized", archives the other session rc=0; a following default-budget pass keeps the quarantine. Full suite on the merged head: 89 passed, 0 failed.
